\section{Conclusions}

Motivated by the problem of representing the large-scale topology of geometric data for the purposes of efficient topological inference and visualization, we have developed a theory for cover learning based on optimization, and an algorithm which outperforms state-of-the-art methods in TDA for both purposes, with easy-to-tune parameters.

We believe that cover learning might be interest to the wider unsupervised learning community, with the potential of having applications in clustering and dimensionality reduction.

This is only a first step towards the development of efficient and robust cover learning methods, opening up several research directions; we mention a few below.
%\luis{opens several research directions}
%\luis{witness complexes deserve more attention: fast implementation (``the ripser for witness complexes'')}

\textit{Limitations and future work.}
Covers produced by cover learning algorithms will often contain spurious intersections, which can render visualization difficult, and lead to incorrect topology recovery.
Robust cover learning, which incorporates density structure, and the development of reliable and interpretable layout algorithms for the nerve of covers are left to future work.

Standard topological inference algorithms, such as Vietoris--Rips, come with topology recovery convergence guarantees (e.g., \cite{kim-shin-chazal-rinaldo-wasserman}), and here we have not provided such guarantees for optimization-based cover learning procedures.
We believe that the nerve theorem can be used to state computable conditions which, when satisfied, guarantee topology recovery of covers, regardless of how they were obtained.

Topological persistence optimization is slow for various reasons, including the fact that the whole dataset is required at each iteration step, precluding the usage of mini-batch for more efficient optimization.
It is an open question whether there exist methods for mini-batch topological regularization 
(i.e., enforcing trivial topology, which is what the nerve theorem requires).
It may be possible to develop such methods using probabilistic algorithms for topological estimation.




