\section{Experimental Setup}
\paragraph{Datasets.}
We selected a total of seven datasets, including four in English: AQUA~\cite{ling2017program} and GSM8K~\cite{cobbe2021training}, which were extracted from Math Word Problem (MWP) datasets, MMLU-Redux (\citealt{gema2024we}); covering various mathematics subjects, including abstract algebra, college-level mathematics, high school mathematics, and elementary mathematics),
Olympiad Bench (English; OlympBench-EN; \citealt{he2024olympiadbench}), focused on text-based olympiad-mathematics problems, and another two Mandarin datasets: GaoKao (MCQ; \citealt{zhang2023evaluating}), Olympiad Bench (Chinese; OlympBench-CN; \citealt{he2024olympiadbench}).
These datasets include multiple-choice questions (AQUA, MMLU-Redux, GaoKao MCQ) and string-valued answers (GSM8K, Olympiad Bench).
The inclusion of complex datasets like GaoKao and Olympiad allows us to assess model performance on academically challenging, non-standard, questions, such as those in Olympiad-level exams and specialised academic tasks. %

\paragraph{Evaluation Metrics.} For multiple-choice datasets, we used exact match metrics, requiring the predicted answer to fully match the correct one, not just the option code (e.g., \textit{A}, \textit{B}). This ensures evaluation based on complete responses, addressing clarity concerns in datasets like MMLU-Redux. For string-valued answers, we also used exact match in GSM8K. On the Olympiad Bench, we used GPT-4o-mini as a benchmark to evaluate how well the model's answers aligned with the ground truth. Further details about GPT-4o-mini evaluation can be found in Appendix~\ref{sec: Implementation Details}.


\paragraph{Language Models.} 
We conducted our experiments using GPT-4o~\cite{achiam2023gpt}, Gemini~\cite{team2023gemini}, and Qwen2~\cite{yang2024qwen2}. This variety allows us to examine performance across different model architectures, sizes, and language proficiencies. For Faithful CoT, we used GPT-4, following the baselines from~\citet{lyu2023faithful}, as GPT-4o tends to produce more invalid outputs compared to GPT-4.

\paragraph{Baselines.} We compared our method against two baselines using the same decoding strategies: greedy decoding, where the most probable next token is selected (temperature = 0.0). The baselines include: (1) Standard Prompting, using a similar prompt to that in Holistic Evaluation of Language Models~\cite{lee2024holistic}; (2) CoT prompting, famous ``Let's think step by step''~\cite{kojima2022large}. For common benchmarks, we included Faithful CoT for additional comparison. In cases where results were not available for specific tasks, we reproduced the baseline results to ensure consistency and fairness in comparison. 

