Our method was more sample-efficient, with superior average score throughout the training, and achieved a significantly better ($p=1.1e{-22}$) score of $910.39$ after training (Fig. \ref{fig:lccar}). Moreover, as  Table \ref{tab:results} shows, it did so by using only $2\%$ the number of tokens per frame as the patch-based solution and $62\%$ less adjustable parameters. And despite running on CPU, it trained 2.7 times faster with respect to the patch-based method, which ran on GPU.

