The effectiveness of the PQG module is validated in Table~\ref{tab:ablt_PQG}.
Rows 1–3 show comparable performance, suggesting that purely learnable queries tend to overfit easy or redundant samples, even when uni-modal priors are available.
Row 4 shows a slight drop, likely due to an excessive number of prior-based queries exceeding the number of ground-truth instances, resulting in more false positives.
In contrast, our design (row 5) assigns strong geometric and texture priors to confident regions, while reserving learnable queries for harder, low-prior cases. This balanced allocation improves the model’s ability to handle both easy and difficult samples, leading to superior overall performance.

