\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$S_1 \cong  \POmegaPlus(2d,3), d \geq 4$}.}\label{POmega-case}

We first use \Cref{thm:ConstructiveRecognition} to obtain an isomorphism ${\rm{Iso}}: S_1 \to \POmegaPlus(2d,3)$, if $S_1 \cong \POmegaPlus(2d,3)$. This step is polynomial-time computable in the setting of quotients and $\textsf{NC}$-computable in the setting of permutation groups. 

Our aim in this case is to determine if $3 \nmid |A|/|S|$ and  $A \not \leq {\rm{PO}}^+(2d,3)$ in the 3rd column. Note that as $A\leq \aut(S_1)$ and $S_1\cong \POmegaPlus(2d,3)$, using ${\rm{Iso}}$, we can view $A \leq \aut(\POmegaPlus(2d,3))$. By  \Cref{Thm-Iso-POmegga-Omega}, we have $A$ as a subgroup of $\aut(\Omega^+(2d,3))$, and hence $A, {\rm{PO}}^+(2d,3) \leq \aut(\Omega^+(2d,3))$. It is known that, in general, there are only inner, diagonal, field and graph automorphisms of the group $\Omega^+(2d,q)$ (cf. \Cref{def:automorphisms}). However, in the case of $\Omega^+(2d,3)$, the field has size $3$. Thus, it does not admit any non-trivial field automorphisms. Also, $\Omega^+(2d,3)$ does not have any graph automorphisms \cite{KleidmanLiebeck}. 

Suppose $X = \begin{pmatrix} 0_{d} & I_{d}\\ I_{d} & 0_{d} \end{pmatrix}$  is the matrix of the symmetric bilinear form fixed by the element of $\rm{O}^+(2d,3)$, then
$\rm{O}^+(2d,3) =\{F \in M_{2d}(3) : FXF^{t}=F \}$ \cite{KleidmanLiebeck,dieudonne1951}. Let $Z=Z({\rm{O}}^+(2d,3)) \cap \Omega^+(2d,3)$. Note that any automorphism of $\Omega^+(2d,3)$ is induced by the projective image of the matrices $F \in \mathrm{GL}(2d,3)$ such that $FX F^{t} = \pm$. Here, we use the fact that ${\rm PO}^{+}(2d,3)={\rm O}^{+}(2d,3)/Z({\rm O}^{+}(2d,3))$ can be viewed as a subgroup of $\aut(\Omega^{+}(2d,3))$, and it contains those automorphisms of $\aut(\Omega^{+}(2d,3))$ that fix the symmetric bilinear form. Namely, let $FZ \in  {\rm PO}^{+}(2d,3)$, and let $\alpha_{F}$ be an induced automorphism obtained from $F$. If $FX F^{t} = X$, then $\alpha_{F} \in {\rm PO}^{+}(2d,3)$. Therefore, to test if $A\leq {\rm{PO}}^+(2d,3)$ it is enough to check if the generators of $A$ are induced by the projective image of $F \in {\rm O}^{+}(2d,3)$.

We now describe the computational details. Let $N_G(S_1)=\langle g_1, \ldots, g_t  \rangle$ and $A=\langle C_{g_{1}}, \ldots, C_{g_{t}} \rangle$. Let $U \in \mathrm{\Omega^{+}}(2d,3)$ with rows and columns of $U$ are numbered by $1,\ldots, d,$ $ -1, \ldots, -d$. Let:
\begin{align*}
L=\{&I+\beta(e_{i,j}-e_{-j,-i}), \\
&I-\beta(e_{-i,-j}-e_{j,i}), \\
&I+\beta(e_{i,-j}-e_{j,-i}), \\
&I+\beta(e_{-i,j}-e_{-j,i})  \mid 0 < i < j, \beta \in \mathbb{F}_3\},    
\end{align*} 
where $e_{i,j}$ is the matrix which has $1$ in the $(i, j)$th entry and $0$ in other places. Then $\mathrm{\Omega^{+}}(2d,3)=\langle L \rangle$ (see e.g., \cite[Page~185]{carter-book}). Note that as $|Z| = 2$, we have that for each $U \in L$, $\ord(U)=3$ and $(|Z|,\ord(U))=1$.


Define $\lambda_{g_{i}} \in \aut(\POmegaPlus(2d,3))$ as $\lambda_{g_{i}}= \mathrm{Iso} \circ C_{g_{i}} \circ \mathrm{Iso}^{-1}$. Thus $A = \langle \lambda_{g_{1}}, \ldots, \lambda_{g_{t}} \rangle \leq \aut(\POmegaPlus(2d,3))$. We apply \Cref{construct-alpha-POmega} to each $\lambda_{g_i}$ to get $\alpha_{g_i} \in \aut(\Omega^+(2d,3))$ such that $\lambda_{g_i}=\bar{\alpha}_{g_i}$. The subgroup $\langle \alpha_{g_1},\ldots, \alpha_{g_t}\rangle$ is the natural embedding of $A$ in $\aut(\Omega^+(2d,3))$.

Note that now it is enough to check if $\alpha_{g_i}$ is in ${\rm{PO}}^+(2d,3)$ for each $i \in [t]$. Equivalently, we want to check if $\alpha_{g_i}$ is induced by an element of ${\rm{PO}}^+(2d,3)$. Fix $i$ and consider the following system of $|L|d^2$ equation for each $\alpha_{g_i}$:
\[
F_{{g_i}}U_j=\alpha_{g_{i}}(U_j)F_{{g_i}}  \text{ for each } 1 \leq j \leq |L|,
\]
where the unknowns are the entries of $F_{{g_i}}$. This system has $d^2|L|$ equation on $d^2$ unknowns. We can solve this system of  equations in $\textsf{NC}^2$ \cite{MulmuleyRank}. By \Cref{lemma-module-equ-cond-POmega}, a non-zero solution to the system always exists. Moreover, if $F_{{g_i}}$ is solution to the above system, then by \cref{thm-schur}, $F_{{g_i}}\in {\rm{GL}}(2d,3)$.  Thus, the automorphism $\alpha_{g_i}$ is induced by the projective image of $F_{{g_i}} \in {\rm{GL}}(2d,
3)$. Further, $\alpha_{g_{i}} \in \mathrm{Aut(\Omega^{+}}(2d,3))$ is in ${\rm{PO}}^{+}(2d,3)$ if and only if the matrix $F_{g_{i}}$, satisfy $F_{g_{i}}X F_{g_{i}}^{t} =X$ i.e., $F_{g_{i}} \in \mathrm{O^{+}}(2d,3)$. 

For each $\alpha_{g_{i}}$ we find the matrix $F_{g_{i}}$ in $\textsf{NC}^2$ as described above. Also, note that $|L|$ is polynomially bounded. For each $F_{g_{i}}$ we check if $F_{g_{i}}X F_{g_{i}}^{T} =X$. Therefore we can determine if $A \not \leq {\rm{PO}}^{+}(2d,3)$, and if $3 \nmid |A/S_1|$ in polynomial-time in the setting of quotients and $\textsf{NC}$ in the setting of permutation groups. If $A \not \leq {\rm{PO}}^{+}(2d,3)$ and $3 \nmid |A/S_1|$ then $\mu(G,N)= \frac{(3^d-1)(3^{d-1}+1)}{2}$, otherwise $\mu(G,N)=\mu(S_1)$ (see e.g., \Cref{MD-AlmostSimple}). 

This completes the case when $S_1\cong \POmegaPlus(2d,3)$.


\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$S_1 \cong \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$, $\mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q}) \in \{\G(3^e), \F(2^e), \Esix(q)\}$}.}\label{sec eceptional groups}


In this section, we handle the remaining simple groups (simple groups of Lie type from rows 14, 15, 16 in \Cref{Table-MD-AlmostSimple}). Our approach deviates slightly than what we have done in the previous sections. The reason is that the exceptional groups have small orders, allowing us to compute their Cayley tables. However, we also do not have the matrix group structure as in earlier cases, which makes the analysis more intricate. We begin by providing an overview of our approach.

We start with the identification of  $S_1$ with $\mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$, i.e. $S_1 \cong \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$. It is well known that any non-abelian simple group has a generating set of size two. Also, the order of the simple group of Lie type is bounded by $n^9$ \cite{liebeck1984minimal}. Consequently, if $S_1 \cong \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$ then $|S_1| \leq n^9$. By Lemma~\ref{PermutationGroupsNC}, we can check if $|S_1| \leq n^9$ in $\textsf{NC}$. Note that if we are in the setting of quotient groups where $S_1 = T/K$ (for some permutation group $T \leq G$), we compute $|S_1|$ by computing $|T|$ and $|K|$ using Lemma~\ref{PermutationGroupsNC}. So suppose $|S_1| \leq n^9$. We first compute its Cayley table in $\textsf{NC}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:List} (this bound holds both in the setting of permutation groups and quotients). Given an access to a Cayley table of $S_1$, we can compute a two size generating set of $S_1$ in $\textsf{NC}$ \cite{Wolf, WagnerThesis, TangThesis}  (actually in $\textsf{L}$ \cite{TangThesis}).

To find the generating set of the group $\mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$, we will leverage the Chevally presentation of the group $\mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$ (cf. \Cref{subsec: simplegroup}). We then try all possible $2$-element subsets of $\mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$ to find a generating set of the group $\mathcal{L}$ satisfying the Chevally presentation. Using the generator-enumeration strategy, we find an isomorphism ${{\rm{Iso}}}: S_1 \rightarrow \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$, if $S_1 \cong \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}_{q})$. Note that, our aim in this case is to determine whether $A \not \leq \Gamma \G(3^e)$ in the 3rd column of \Cref{Table-MD-AlmostSimple}. We have $A, \Gamma \G(3^e) \leq \aut(\G(3^e))$, and $\aut(\G(3^e)) \setminus \Gamma \G(3^e)$ are exactly those automorphisms which contain a  graph automorphism in their decompositions. Thus it is enough to check if each generator of $A$ is free of graph automorphism.



We now describe the computational details. We show the computation for the case when $S_1 \cong \G(3^e)$, and rest of the cases follow similarly.

Let $N_G(S_1) = \langle g_1, \ldots, g_t \rangle$ and $A=\langle C_{g_{1}},\ldots, C_{g_{t}}\rangle$. Define $\lambda_{g_{i}} \in \aut(\mathcal{L}(\mathbb{F}))$ as $\lambda_{g_{i}}= \mathrm{Iso} \circ C_{g_{i}} \circ \mathrm{Iso}^{-1}$. Since $\aut(S_1)= \aut(\G(3^e))$ we have $A=\langle \lambda_{g_{1}},\ldots, \lambda_{g_{t}}\rangle \leq  \aut(\G(3^e))$ using an isomorphism, Iso.

For each $\lambda_{g_{i}}$, define $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}=\lambda_{g_{i}}\mathbbm{f}_{\sigma_0}^{-t'}\mathbbm{g}^{-t''}$ for $1 \leq t'\leq e$ and $t'' \in \{0,1\}$. Now we want to check the type of $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}$ which will be used to identify the type of $\lambda_{g_{i}}$. For each $i$, and each $t',t''$ we check if $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}$ is either an inner automorphism or a diagonal automorphism by checking its value at all the elements of a group. This can be done since we have a Cayley table of $\G(3^e)$. Thus we can identify the type $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}$. For a fix $i$, there are $2e$ possible $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}$. It is easy to see that out of these $2e$ possibilities, there is exactly one choice of $t'$ and $t''$ such that $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}$ is either an inner or a diagonal automorphism. 

Suppose we identify that $\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}$ is an inner or a diagonal automorphism for some $t',t''$. If $t''=0$ then we get  $\lambda_{g_{i}}=\alpha_{g_{i}}^{t',t''}  \mathbbm{f}_{\sigma_0}^{t'}$ and $\lambda_{g_{i}} \in \Gamma \G(3^e)$. Thus for each $\lambda_{g_{i}}$ we can test if $\lambda_{g_{i}} \in \Gamma \G(3^e)$. Hence we can identify if $A \not \leq \Gamma \G(3^e)$. If $A  \leq \Gamma \G(3^e)$ then $\mu(G,N)=\mu(S_1)$ otherwise $\mu(G,N)=2\mu(S_1)$ (see e.g., Lemma 2.4, \cite{CannonHoltUnger}).


Note that we can write each Chevalley generators explicitly and we can also check if the automorphism is inner or a diagonal in polynomial-time in the setting of quotients by and in $\textsf{NC}$ in the setting of permutation groups. The case when $S_1$ is isomorphic to either $\F(2^e)$ or $ \Esix(q)$ is handled similarly as above -- we use in place of $\G(3^e)$ and $\Gamma\G(3^e)$ either: $\F(2^e)$ and $\Gamma\F(2^e)$; or $\Esix(q)$ and $\Gamma\Esix(q)$. The proof then goes through \emph{mutatis mutandis}.

Therefore, we can handle each row of the \Cref{Table-MD-AlmostSimple}, and check the corresponding condition from the 3rd column in in polynomial-time in the setting of quotients by and in $\textsf{NC}$ in the setting of permutation groups. This concludes the proof of \Cref{thm: semisimple_Unique Minimal}. 





