\section{Circuit Complexity of \algprobm{Min-Per-Deg}}

Das and Thakkar \cite{DasThakkarMPD} previously showed that, in the multiplication table model, $\algprobm{Min-Per-Deg} \in \textsf{DSPACE}(\log^3 n)$. In the conclusion, they remark (without proof) that $\algprobm{Graph Isomorphism}$ is not $\textsf{AC}^{0}$-reducible to $\algprobm{Min-Per-Deg}$. Indeed this easily follows from existing techniques \cite{ChattopadhyayToranWagner, CollinsGrochowLevetWeiss}. In this section, we will supply these details. We will begin by recalling some additional preliminaries. 

\subsection{Additional Preliminaries: Time and Space-Restricted Turing Machines}

When considering complexity classes defined by Turing machines with a time bound $t(n) \in o(n)$, we use Turing machines with random access and a separate address (or \emph{index}) tape. After writing an address, the machine can go to a query state reading the symbol from the input at the location specified by the address tape. As usual, the machines are otherwise allowed to have multiple work tapes. 

For functions $t(n), s(n) \in \Omega(\log n)$, the classes $\DTISP(t(n),s(n)) $ and $\NTISP(t(n),s(n)) $ are defined to consist of decision problems computable by deterministic (resp.\ nondeterministic) $t(n)$ time and $s(n)$ space bounded Turing machines. Be aware that there must be one Turing machine that simultaneously satisfies the time and space bound. For details we refer to \cite[Section 2.6]{VollmerText} and \cite{CollinsGrochowLevetWeiss}.



\subsection{Main Result}

Here, we will establish the following:

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:ImprovedDSPACE}
In the multiplication table model, $\algprobm{Min-Per-Deg} \in \exists^{\log^3 n} \ACz(\DTISPpll)$.    
\end{theorem}

We note that \cite[Fact~2.6]{CollinsGrochowLevetWeiss}:
\[
\exists^{\log^3 n} \ACz(\DTISPpll) \subseteq \exists^{\log^3 n} \LogSpace \cap \exists^{\log^3 n} \FOLL \cap \qACz.
\]

Wolf \cite[Lemma~3.1]{Wolf} established that $\exists^{\log^3} \LogSpace \subseteq \DSPACE(\log^3 n)$. Thus, Theorem~\ref{thm:ImprovedDSPACE} improves upon the previous result of Das and Thakkar \cite{DasThakkarMPD}. Theorem~\ref{thm:ImprovedDSPACE} also yields the following corollary.

\begin{corollary}
There is no Turing reduction, from $\algprobm{Graph Isomorphism}$ to $\algprobm{Min-Per-Deg}$ in the multiplication table model, that is computable using $\textsf{quasiAC}$ circuits of depth $o(\log n/\log \log n)$ even when allowing for $\textsf{MOD}$-$p$ gates.   
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Fix primes $p \neq q$. We first note that there exists an $\ACz$-computable, many-one reduction from \algprobm{MOD}-$q$ to \algprobm{Graph Isomorphism} \cite{Toran} (an $\ACz$-computable, many-one reduction from \algprobm{Parity} to \algprobm{Graph Isomorphism} was given previously in \cite{CFI}). On the other hand, $\textsf{quasiAC}$ circuits of depth $o(\log n/\log \log n)$ cannot compute $\textsf{MOD}$-$q$, even in the presence of $\textsf{MOD}$-$p$ gates \cite{Razborov, Smolensky87algebraicmethods}. The result now follows.
\end{proof}

We now turn to proving Theorem~\ref{thm:ImprovedDSPACE}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:ImprovedDSPACE}]
Let $(G, k)$ be an instance of $\algprobm{Min-Per-Deg}$. In order to compute $\mu(G)$, it suffices to consider an appropriate set of at most $k := \log n$ subgroups of $G$. We will guess such a set non-deterministically. Each such subgroup can be specified by a cube generating sequence of size at most $O(\log n)$ \cite{BabaiSzemeredi}, and we require $O(\log n)$ bits to specify a given group element. Thus, we may use $O(\log^3 n)$ existentially quantified non-deterministic bits to guess cube generating sequences for a collection of at most $\log n$ subgroups of $G$, which we call $\mathcal{H} := \{H_{1}, \ldots, H_{k}\}$. As per the proof of \cite[Theorem~4.1]{CollinsGrochowLevetWeiss}, we may in $\ACz(\DTISPpll)$ use the cube generating sequences to write down the elements of $H_1, \ldots, H_k$. 

In $\ACz$, we may evaluate $\sum_{i=1}^{k} [G : H_{i}]$. We will now show that we can decide in $\ACz$ whether:
\[
\bigcap_{i=1}^{k} \text{Core}_{G}(H_{i}) = 1.
\]
To this end, we follow the strategy of Das and Thakkar \cite[Theorem~6.3]{DasThakkarMPD}. Let $x \in G$, and $H \leq G$. We have that $x \in \text{Core}_G(H)$ if and only if $gxg^{-1} \in H$ for all $g \in G$. For each $x \in G$ and each $i \in [k]$, define the indicator $Y(x, i) = 1$ precisely if $x \in \text{Core}_{G}(H_{i})$. For each $x \in G, i \in [k]$, we may compute $Y(x, i)$ in $\ACz$. 
Now 
\[
x \in \bigcap_{i=1}^{k} \text{Core}_{G}(H_{i}) \,  \text{  if and only if } \bigwedge_{i=1}^{k} Y(X,i) = 1.
\]
Thus, for a given $x \in G$, we may decide in $\ACz$ if:
\[
x \in \bigcap_{i=1}^{k} \text{Core}_{G}(H_{i}).
\]
It follows that we can decide in $\ACz$ whether:
\[
\bigcap_{i=1}^{k} \text{Core}_{G}(H_{i}) = 1.
\]
The result now follows.
\end{proof}




\section{Conclusion}

We investigated the computational complexity of \algprobm{Min-Per-Deg} for Fitting-free groups. When the groups are given as quotients of two permutation groups, we established an upper bound of $\textsf{P}$. Furthermore, in the setting of permutation groups, we established an upper bound of $\textsf{NC}$. In the process, we exhibited an upper bound of $\textsf{NC}$ for computing the socle of a Fitting-free group, in the setting of permutation groups. Our work leaves open several problems. The most natural open problem is the following.

\begin{problem} \label{prob:p1}
In the setting of quotients, does \algprobm{Min-Per-Deg} for Fitting-free groups belong to $\textsf{NC}$?
\end{problem}

Resolving Problem~\ref{prob:p1} will likely require novel parallel solutions for much of the polynomial-time framework for quotients developed by Kantor and Luks \cite{KantorLuksQuotients}. We highlight key obstacles next.

\begin{problem}
Let $G, K \leq \Sym(n)$ with $K \trianglelefteq G$, and let $\textbf{G} := G/K$. Let $\textbf{N} \trianglelefteq \textbf{G}$. Compute the centralizer $C_{\textbf{G}}(\textbf{N})$ in $\textsf{NC}$.   
\end{problem}

In order to obtain a polynomial-time solution for computing $C_{\textbf{G}}(\textbf{N})$ in the setting of quotients, Kantor and Luks \cite{KantorLuksQuotients} reduce to computing an appropriate core. Thus, we ask the following.

\begin{problem} \label{prob:Cores}
Let $G, K \leq \Sym(n)$ with $K \trianglelefteq G$, and let $\textbf{G} := G/K$. Let $\textbf{H} \leq \textbf{G}$. Compute $\text{Core}_{\textbf{G}}(\textbf{H})$ in $\textsf{NC}$.    
\end{problem}

Note that Problem~\ref{prob:Cores} is open even in the setting of permutation groups ($K = 1$).

\begin{problem}
Let $G, K \leq \Sym(n)$ with $K \trianglelefteq G$, and let $\textbf{G} := G/K$. Suppose that $\textbf{G}$ is Fitting-free. Compute $\Soc(\textbf{G})$ in $\textsf{NC}$.
\end{problem}

More generally, we ask about computing $\Soc(\textbf{G})$ in $\textsf{NC}$ when $\textbf{G}$ is \emph{not} assumed to be Fitting-free. This is open, even in the setting of permutation groups ($K = 1$).

Theorem~\ref{thm:MainFittingFree}(a) formalizes that much of the framework established by Cannon and Holt \cite{CH03} is polynomial-time computable. In order to strengthen these results to the setting of $\textsf{NC}$, it is necessary but not sufficient to resolve Problem~\ref{prob:p1}. We also need to compute the solvable radical.

\begin{problem} \label{prob:Radical}
Let $G, K \leq \Sym(n)$ with $K \trianglelefteq G$, and let $\textbf{G} := G/K$. Compute $\rad(\textbf{G})$ in $\textsf{NC}$.
\end{problem}

Problem~\ref{prob:Radical} is open, even in the setting of permutation groups ($K = 1$).


