    \caption{\tim{Schematic of the proposed loss-based regularization applied to the FedAvg-based HuBERT ASR system. For simplicity, one client is shown as an example. Darker-colored components represent the global model, while lighter-colored components correspond to the local model.}}
    \label{fig:FL_reg_loss}
\end{figure}

\tim{The embedding-based regularization and loss-based regularization can be applied at multiple positions in the model. Furthermore, as these three regularization techniques operate at different levels, their usage can be combined.}



% This study focuses on the generalization category in federated learning and proposes two algorithms. The first algorithm is called federated learning with multi-position embedding-based regularization, which makes full use of the intermediate embedding-based regularization to tackle the heterogeneity issue. The second algorithm is federated learning with multi-position loss-based regularization, which makes full use of the loss-based regularization to tackle the heterogeneity issue. The proposed algorithms are different from the existing federated learning algorithms focusing on users with Alzheimer’s disease (AD), such as cluster-based personalized FL (CPFL), which belong to the personalization category. Besides, the cluster-based personalized FL (CPFL) suffers from high communication costs since it needs to maintain several models in each client and several models in the server. The communication cost is proportional to the number of models in each client, and communication cost is a critical performance issue in federated learning. 

