\begin{abstract}%\vspace{-0.3cm}
In this article, we introduce a novel low-altitude wireless network (LAWN), which is a reconfigurable, three-dimensional (3D) layered architecture. In particular, the LAWN integrates connectivity, sensing, control, and computing across aerial and terrestrial nodes that enable seamless operation in complex, dynamic, and mission-critical environments. Different from the conventional aerial communication systems, LAWN’s distinctive feature is its tight integration of functional planes in which multiple functionalities continually reshape themselves to operate safely and efficiently in the low-altitude sky. With the LAWN, we discuss several enabling technologies, such as integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), semantic communication, and fully-actuated control systems. Finally, we identify potential applications and key cross-layer challenges. This article offers a comprehensive roadmap for future research and development in the low-altitude airspace.
%As the global demand for intelligent aerial services continues to grow, researchers have turned their attention to the low-altitude airspace between $100$ - $3,000$ meters above ground as a new frontier for digital infrastructure. In this article, we discuss the concept of a Low-Altitude Wireless Network (LAWN), which is established based on a reconfigurable, three-dimensional (3D) architecture. The network integrates connectivity, sensing, control, and computing across aerial and terrestrial nodes that enable seamless operation in complex, dynamic, and mission-critical environments. In particular, we present LAWN's layered architecture by highlighting its distinctive features that set it apart from conventional aerial communication systems, including its tight integration of functional planes. Building on this architecture, we discuss several enabling technologies, such as integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), semantic communication, and fully-actuated control systems. Finally, we identify potential applications and key cross-layer challenges. This article offers a comprehensive roadmap for future research and development in the low-altitude airspace.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Low-altitude wireless networks, data transmission, environmental sensing, low-latency control.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
%800

The low-altitude airspace, roughly below $3,000$m above ground level, has rapidly evolved into a third dimension for digital infrastructure supporting logistics, agriculture, and public safety. It is projected that by 2030, the commercial drone sector will have a value of USD $\$260$ billion, four times higher than today's state\cite{dronemarket}. In parallel, regulators are clearing the skies: the U.S. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA)’s Beyond-Visual-Line-of-Sight rule-making committee issued final recommendations in 2024 that pave the way for routine long-range flights without direct pilot oversight, while Europe’s U-space framework harmonized cooperative traffic management at $120$m above ground level (AGL). At the same time, the civil aviation administration of China (CAAC) made advanced air-mobility and “low-altitude economy” a national plan, charting a phased roadmap for exploiting low-altitude airspace. The convergence of market growth, permissive regulation, and maturing autonomy is giving rise to the low-altitude wireless network (LAWN). By eliminating the constraint of fixed ground stations and enabling rapid topology re-configuration, LAWNs promise various capabilities for precision agriculture, distributed aerial sensing, swarm-based disaster response, and the broader low-altitude economy, as shown in Fig. \ref{scenario}.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{01.pdf}
    \caption{The low-altitude wireless network (LAWN) framework integrates a diverse ecosystem of aerial platforms operating below 3,000 meters to enable a multi-functional, reconfigurable 3D network. The control and non-payload communication (CNPC) links ensure reliable control, the payload communication signals handle the transmission of mission-specific data, and the sensing signals enable active environmental perception.}
    \label{scenario}
\end{figure}

While aerial communications have been investigated extensively in the last decade, the LAWN is more than the concept of “cellular-in-the-sky” \cite{zeng2019accessing}. That is, the LAWN is a flexible, three-dimensional web that integrates connectivity, real-time sensing, and closed-loop control across multiple drones and terrestrial nodes. Unlike terrestrial or high-altitude systems, the operational environment for the LAWN is usually densely cluttered (e.g., $100$ aerial drones per km$^2$) and rapidly evolving. For instance, multiple drones collaborate to execute specific tasks and may take various actions such as climbing, hovering, and banking at up to $30$m/s velocity. Moreover, the clear line-of-sight (LOS) drone-to-drone or drone-to-ground links may translate into non-line-of-sight (NLOS) behind buildings. Therefore, the signal paths, interference patterns, and safety envelopes may change in a very short time duration. Moreover, the flight control often demands end-to-end latency below $10$ms with $99.999$\% reliability to achieve the stability\cite{tischler2018system}. To satisfy the combined needs of joint connectivity-sensing–control and even computing, a LAWN is expected to be elastic in both topology and function: the drones within range should be capable of sharing information without human control; the radio waveform carrying data may be used for radar sensing such that the drone can ``see'' obstacles while it talks; the on-board drone controllers could reroute or re-plan the swarm formation to combat environmental dynamics, and allocate computing resources between drones and edge servers for balancing battery consumptions. Essentially, a LAWN is a cross-disciplinary concept in which multiple functionalities continually reshape themselves to operate safely and efficiently in the low-altitude sky.

Despite the great potential of LAWN for the 6G ecosystem, most prior works tackle isolated topics such as drone-to-ground communication and trajectory design. This article fills this gap by offering the first end-to-end perspective on what a LAWN is, why it matters, and how it can be realized. In particular, we commence from the architectures of a LAWN by presenting its functional planes, i.e., data transmission, control, sensing, and computing, clarifying where it diverges from traditional aerial communications or drone-assisted mesh networks. Next, we highlight several enabling technologies, such as integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) and artificial intelligence (AI) and potential applications, e.g., precision agriculture related to the LAWN. Then, a case study of swarm coordination in post-disaster scenarios is discussed. To promote the realization of the LAWN, we summarize various challenges, such as 3D spectrum management, cyber-physical security, and regulations. Finally, we draw the conclusions. %We hope that this paper would serve as a tutorial for researchers in this area and inspire low-altitude innovations.
