\section{Enabling Technologies and Potential Applications}

\subsection{Enabling Technologies}
The realization of LAWNs relies on a diverse set of enabling technologies spanning from physical-layer waveform design, intelligent control, to network-level intelligence, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:enabling}. While the architecture of a LAWN is detailed in Sec. II, advanced tools and protocols are essential to unlock its full potential. 



\subsubsection{Integrated Sensing and Communications (ISAC)}
ISAC is a transformative technology that allows performing both wireless communication and environmental sensing simultaneously by using the same signaling, hardware platform, and radio resources\cite{liu2022integrated}. Traditionally, sensing and communication have been treated as distinct functions. However, in a LAWN, where drones continuously exchange information while also perceiving the dynamic environments, ISAC offers a highly efficient solution. By eliminating the need for separate sensing and communication modules, ISAC reduces spectrum usage and hardware complexity of the aerial nodes in LAWNs. As an example, a drone transmitting signals to a ground node can also monitor the reflected echoes from objects to detect emerging obstacles or track other drones without switching to a dedicated radar or LiDAR system. The received echoes at the LAWN nodes are processed via radar processing, like matched filtering and beamforming, followed by detection and estimation. With multiple drones acting as sensors over a wide area, the LAWN yields a large aperture, improving angular resolution and imaging capabilities. ISAC also facilitates channel state information acquisition via sensing rather than classical pilot-based channel estimation, further enhancing the system efficiency. Nevertheless, integrating ISAC into LAWN nodes imposes challenges such as waveform, hardware, and antenna designs. 


\subsubsection{Delay-Doppler Waveform}
Delay-Doppler (DD) waveforms, including orthogonal time frequency space (OTFS) and its variants, were proposed in 2017 for reliable data transmission in highly dynamic wireless channels. Unlike conventional time-frequency (TF) domain modulation, OTFS modulates symbols in the two-dimensional DD domain\cite{yuan2023new}. Since the physical world merely experiences a slight change in a short time duration, it is expected that the DD parameters associated with the channel are almost unchanged during a short symbol interval. Relying on the DD channel representation, OTFS converts the time-varying channels into near time-invariant ones, providing significant resilience when the channels suffer from high Doppler shifts and rapid channel fluctuations.  Equalization in this domain becomes simpler; even if the drone or the scatterers are moving, their effect is concentrated in a few DD bins over the packet duration.  Furthermore, DD waveform leverages full path diversity (all reflected delays/Dopplers) to maximize throughput under motion. Importantly, the DD waveform inherently carries physical meaning: a symbol’s spreading in the time-frequency signal corresponds to probing a range of delays and Dopplers. Thus, a LAWN receiver can naturally extract range and velocity information of reflecting objects from the communication signal itself. However, the 2D transforms involved can be computationally intensive for large signal bandwidths or high-resolution delay-Doppler grids, which is challenging for maintaining low-latency links. 




\subsubsection{Semantic Communications}
Rather than accurate bit-level transmission, semantic communications move the focus to successful delivery of task-relevant meaning while discarding bit redundancy. Instead of reconstructing transmitted messages with minimal bit error, semantic communication systems seek to understand the context, intent, and importance of the transmitted information, transmitting only the elements that directly affect the receiver's task or inference. In LAWNs, a semantic-aware node may extract high-level descriptions rather than raw sensor data and transmit only the essential semantic representations, as discussed in the interest-based image segment transmission scheme\cite{10475844  }. This greatly reduces bandwidth consumption and energy usage, which is especially valuable for power-constrained drones. For example, rather than streaming a full 4K video feed, a drone may send a concise semantic message such as ``three pedestrians crossing in two seconds,'' allowing other LAWN nodes to react without processing the raw visual data. Semantic awareness also allows smarter control decisions. The controller can prioritize flows based on semantic importance, dynamically allocating bandwidth where it yields most task gain. To deploy semantic communications in the LAWN, the resource-constrained drones must run relatively sophisticated AI to interpret raw data. Another challenge is trust and correctness since some details are intentionally lost. In critical applications, an incorrect or oversimplified semantic message could lead to wrong decisions.

\subsubsection{Artificial Intelligence and Large Language Models}
AI is indeed the backbone technology in enabling the autonomy and intelligence of the LAWN \cite{zhao2025generative}. It is closely connected with the capabilities for perception, decision-making, and cooperative behavior across spatially distributed nodes. By deploying lightweight neural networks on drones or edge servers, a wide range of real-time tasks such as channel prediction, trajectory optimization, obstacle classification, and intent estimation can be supported. The drones can be trained to navigate through complex environments or select optimal relay positions through reinforcement learning algorithms. The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has offered a new dimension of cognitive functionality, enabling reasoning, instruction interpretation, and symbolic decision-making. When integrated into LAWNs, LLM can translate natural-language instructions into structured mission parameters, enabling intuitive command interfaces for non-expert users. In turn, the mission logs, sensor feeds, or visual data obtained by the LAWN can be summarized as natural-language reports for post-mission review. Furthermore, LLM may assist semantic communications within multi-agent coordination through shared symbolic understanding\cite{zhao2025generative}. Ensuring real-time performance of AI algorithms is crucial. Techniques like model compression, quantization, hardware acceleration (using onboard GPUs or NPUs), and offloading computation to edge servers are all employed to make AI feasible for resource-constrained drones. Moreover, the LAWN should incorporate online learning or periodic retraining to ensure that models stay robust over time.

\subsubsection{Edge Intelligence}
The LAWN architecture includes a hierarchical computing plane: drones handle time-critical, lightweight tasks; edge servers manage latency-sensitive, compute-heavy functions; and cloud nodes perform long-term analytics. This hierarchy enables dynamic task partitioning\cite{kaleem2019uav}. For example, a drone may offload an intensive task like high-resolution image recognition to an edge server, while keeping tasks like simple obstacle avoidance on-board. A key advancement is edge perception, wherein rich, multimodal sensor data from multiple drones is transmitted to edge nodes for high-level scene understanding. Edge servers run deep models to generate real-time situational awareness and distribute the perception results back to the swarm. This approach enables each drone to benefit from a global perception system while minimizing onboard processing and inter-drone communication. A significant challenge in achieving edge intelligence in LAWNs is the 3D mobility, which causes intermittent connectivity and demands seamless handoff of tasks across edge nodes. 

\subsubsection{Fully-Actuated Control Systems}
Traditional drones often rely on state-space control models that work well for linear systems. However, these approaches may become inadequate in dynamic, uncertain, or time-varying environments. The fully actuated system (FAS) approach offers a new control paradigm that enables precise, robust, and real-time control design for highly nonlinear and time-varying systems\cite{10689714}. The FAS approach constructs a control-oriented model structure that allows the control input to be explicitly solved, bypassing the need for standard state-space variable extensions. By converting the original physical model into a mathematically equivalent fully actuated form, the FAS framework enables direct controller design with arbitrarily assignable system dynamics. In the LAWN, aerial nodes modeled using the FAS framework can be stabilized via linear or nonlinear control laws that directly assign desired performance, even in the presence of modeling uncertainty, communication delays, and external disturbances. Moreover, FAS provides effective control even for systems that are not feedback linearizable, making it highly suitable for uncertain aerial dynamics in low-altitude environment. With its theoretical guarantees, fully-actuated control systems strengthen the autonomy, agility, and safety of LAWNs.

%\subsection{Wireless Power Transfer}
%Energy supply is one of the most critical constraints for LAWN. Traditional drones require frequent landings for recharging, which restricts mission continuity. Wireless power transfer (WPT) enables drones to refuel energy in-flight or during hover without physical contact\cite{xie2021uav}. Near-field wireless charging methods allow drones to recharge while hovering over rooftops. For longer-range scenarios, far-field WPT using microwave or millimeter-wave beams can deliver energy to drones in-flight. By cooperating with the control and computing planes, the LAWN can guide drones toward optimal recharging points based on battery status, task priorities, and routing distances. 

