corners.

\begin{theorem}[Improved outer bound]
The refined Q cell $Q^*_\x$ is a tighter outer bound on $C_\x$ than $Q_\x$,
\ie,
\begin{equation}
   C_\x \subseteq Q^*_\x \subseteq Q_\x \quad \forall \x\in\calX,
   \label{ref_bound}
\end{equation}
and $\calC\subseteq\calQ^*\subseteq\calQ$.
\label{thm:main2}
\end{theorem}
\begin{IEEEproof}
First inclusion: Since $Q^{(2)}_\x$ is convex, the intersection with a tangential half-plane at any point of its boundary
leaves it unchanged. So $Q^{(2)}_\x\subseteq Q^*_\x$, which implies $C_\x\subseteq Q^*_\x$.
Second inclusion: Since the corner points of $Q^*_\x$ lie inside $Q_\x$, the intersection with $\calH_\x^\flat$
makes $Q^*_\x$ smaller than $Q_\x$---unless there are no corner points.
\end{IEEEproof}
If there are more than two interferers, the first inclusion in \eqref{ref_bound} becomes strict, and
if $Q_\x(\rho^*)$ has at least two corner points, the second inclusion becomes strict.

