By Definition \ref{def-place-bis}, place bisimilarity $\sim_p$ can be defined as follows:

$\sim_p = \bigcup \{ R^\oplus \mid R \mbox{ is a place bisimulation}\}.$

\noindent
However, it is not true that 

$\sim_p = (\bigcup \{ R \mid R \mbox{ is a place bisimulation}\})^\oplus$

\noindent 
as the union of place bisimulations may not be a place bisimulation \cite{ABS91,Gor21decid,Gor25a}, 
so that its definition is not coinductive. 
Hence, the polynomial algorithms for checking bisimulation equivalence over LTSs (e.g., \cite{KS90,PT87})
cannot be adapted to compute $\sim_p$. Nonetheless, in \cite{Gor21decid,Gor25a} it is proved that $\sim_p$
can be decided in exponential time over the whole class of finite P/T Petri nets; a hint at this decidability proof is given in Section \ref{case-sec}.



